  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A nice end to the Labor Day weekend.\n\nA few clouds outside this afternoon, but a very dry airmass \nis above about 850 mb, so no showers for a change after a wet \nstart to September. \n\nIn the wake of Saturday's cold front, a refreshingly low\ndewpoint airmass has settled in across Pennsylvania and will\nensure pleasant conditions through Labor Day. \n\nHigh pressure will build overhead tonight, with calm winds,\nclear skies, and antecedent moisture supporting widespread\nvalley fog development by Monday morning. Lows will range from\nthe mid 40s in the northwest mountains to mid 50s in the\nsoutheast. No doubt a perfect night to open the windows!\n\nAfter morning fog mixes out, Labor Day will be about as good as\nit gets. A few wispy cirrus clouds will accentuate bright blue\nskies with abundant sunshine, passing fair weather cumulus\nclouds, and seasonable temperatures. \n\n   ----------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Uptick in heat and humidity for midweek.\n\nAs high pressure drifts southeast of PA by Wednesday, \na southwesterly flow will usher in warmer and a bit more\nhumid airmass for later Wednesday. Most of the day will be\ndry. \n\nWind fields will be on the strong side, but dewpoints\nstay below 70 degrees, and frontal timing is late Wednesday\nnight early Thursday, thus the risk of severe storms limited \nat this point.\n\nWhile temperatures will be above normal on Wednesday, nothing\nlike what we just had last week. \n\n   ----------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Showers, storms, and gusty winds Wednesday night \ninto Thursday.\n\nBy Wednesday night, a low pressure system will be moving across\nnorthern Ontario with a cold front extending south through the \nGreat Lakes and Ohio Valley region. The pressure gradient \nwill support a notable uptick in synoptic scale winds Wednesday\nnight into Thursday. It's been awhile since we've had widespread\ngusty winds, but we're getting into the time of year where such\na weather pattern becomes more common. \n\nGusty winds will ramp up Wednesday during the day out of the\nsouthwest ahead of an approaching cold front, and continue\nthrough Thursday afternoon shifting to a westerly direction \nafter the front passes. Wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph seem \nrealistic at this point, with the highest readings occurring at\nhigher elevations in the Laurel Highlands.\n\nHighest POPS for showers and storms will be on Thursday,\nas front moves into southern PA.\n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front and the end of the\nweek/start of the weekend looks sunny and dry with low humidity.",
